    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_df9a2cd0c13777e9b69f8705228ba95df6acffa62baa6d6c79491363f9d1377e",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "A common leading Gaussian fluctuation scale is insufficient to identify family symmetry. Lower-order diagnostics remain distinct: prime-square survival can shift the center of log central values; Gamma and Barnes-G combinations retain ensemble-specific moment constants; persistent central zeros and root-number parity add separate low-zero channels; and a trace-formula weight changes the averaging measure and may distort the observed density relative to the unweighted family.",
